Assistant: The signaling value of central bank communications depends on several key factors. First, the credibility of the central bank, built through past consistency between statements and actions, determines how much weight market participants place on forward guidance. Second, the precision and clarity of the communication affects interpretability - more specific guidance reduces uncertainty but may constrain future flexibility. Third, the economic context matters, as communications have greater impact when uncertainty is high or when conventional policy tools are constrained. Empirical studies show that central bank communications significantly affect asset prices and economic expectations, though effects vary across communication types and economic conditions. The optimal communication strategy involves balancing transparency benefits against the need to maintain policy flexibility.

