User: What determines the targeting efficiency of categorical programs?
Assistant: The targeting efficiency of categorical programs depends on several key factors. First, the correlation between observable categorical characteristics (age, disability status, family structure) and underlying need determines the program's ability to identify intended beneficiaries. Second, administrative costs and complexity affect both take-up rates among eligible populations and the resources available for benefits. Third, the precision of eligibility determination involves tradeoffs between Type I errors (excluding eligible individuals) and Type II errors (including ineligible individuals), with optimal thresholds depending on the relative social costs of each error type. Empirical evidence suggests wide variation in targeting efficiency across programs, with means-tested transfers generally achieving higher target efficiency ratios than universal categorical programs, though at the cost of lower take-up rates and higher administrative burdens.

