Assistant: Reimbursement schedules affect healthcare service supply through both price and non-price mechanisms. Higher reimbursement rates increase the quantity supplied along the supply curve, while also affecting provider entry and exit decisions in the long run. The elasticity of supply varies considerably across service types, with emergency services exhibiting lower responsiveness than elective procedures. Evidence suggests that fee-for-service systems tend to increase service volume, while capitated or bundled payment systems may reduce utilization but potentially affect quality margins. The net welfare effects depend on whether baseline provision levels are above or below the social optimum, which varies by service type and market structure.

